# Reviewed findings, one gitleaks fingerprint per line.
#
# The scan runs with --no-git (see the "Scan for committed secrets" step in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml), so it examines the working tree rather than a
# range of commits. Fingerprints are therefore `file:rule:line` — no commit
# hash. A history-pinned fingerprint from the old commit-range scan would not
# match, in either repository.
#
# Why the scan changed: the action's default is to diff `<before>^..<after>`,
# which cannot work when the first commit is in that range — a root commit has
# no parent. The distribution repository's history is rewritten by every
# publish, so its root commit keeps moving and that scan kept failing having
# read nothing at all. Scanning the tree asks the question that actually
# matters for generated code: does what this repository contains right now hold
# a secret?
#
# The entry that used to live here covered a smoke-test administrator password
# hard-coded in the workflow; it is generated per run now, so the finding no
# longer exists to ignore. Add a line here only for a finding somebody has read
# and judged safe, with the reason written above it.

